1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,233
A gas flame heats a pan.

2
00:00:02,233 --> 00:00:03,907
A bulb lights the room.

3
00:00:03,907 --> 00:00:05,720
A pressure cooker whistles.

4
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:07,599
Heat, light and sound.

5
00:00:07,599 --> 00:00:09,482
Three different forms of energy.

6
00:00:09,482 --> 00:00:11,014
Where do they come from?

7
00:00:11,464 --> 00:00:12,943
Diya is cooking dinner.

8
00:00:12,943 --> 00:00:16,513
The gas burns, the pan gets
hot, and the cooker whistles.

9
00:00:16,863 --> 00:00:20,058
Then the stove goes off,
and the warmth fades.

10
00:00:20,058 --> 00:00:23,363
Was the energy used up,
or did it go somewhere?

11
00:00:23,863 --> 00:00:27,755
By the end, you will know the one
rule that energy never breaks.

12
00:00:28,405 --> 00:00:30,427
So what are the forms of energy?

13
00:00:30,777 --> 00:00:33,167
Energy is the ability to do work.

14
00:00:33,167 --> 00:00:34,919
It comes in many forms.

15
00:00:35,319 --> 00:00:37,530
Moving things have kinetic energy.

16
00:00:37,530 --> 00:00:40,646
Raised or stretched things
store potential energy.

17
00:00:41,046 --> 00:00:43,002
Hot things have heat energy.

18
00:00:43,002 --> 00:00:45,420
Fuel and food store chemical energy.

19
00:00:45,820 --> 00:00:50,491
There is also electrical energy,
light, sound, and nuclear energy.

20
00:00:50,891 --> 00:00:53,964
And energy keeps changing
from one form to another.

21
00:00:53,964 --> 00:00:57,460
Chemical energy in the gas
becomes heat, light and sound.

22
00:00:58,110 --> 00:01:02,234
A two kilogram block sits on a
rough ramp, five metres high.

23
00:01:02,584 --> 00:01:07,746
At the top, it stores ninety eight
joules of potential energy, m g h.

24
00:01:08,146 --> 00:01:09,070
Let it go.

25
00:01:09,070 --> 00:01:13,757
As it slides, the potential energy
falls, and the kinetic energy grows.

26
00:01:14,157 --> 00:01:17,110
But friction rubs the
block against the ramp.

27
00:01:17,110 --> 00:01:19,043
Some energy turns into heat.

28
00:01:19,443 --> 00:01:23,980
At the bottom, fifty eight joules of
kinetic energy, and forty joules of

29
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:24,880
heat.

30
00:01:25,028 --> 00:01:27,245
On the graph, three lines change.

31
00:01:27,245 --> 00:01:30,383
But the total stays flat,
at ninety eight joules.

32
00:01:30,883 --> 00:01:33,058
Slow-mo replay, halfway down.

33
00:01:33,408 --> 00:01:37,082
Friction pushes back on the
block, and warms both surfaces.

34
00:01:37,082 --> 00:01:39,013
Not a single joule disappears.

35
00:01:39,663 --> 00:01:42,438
Now let's write the rule, step by step.

36
00:01:42,788 --> 00:01:43,688
Step one.

37
00:01:43,593 --> 00:01:45,763
Friction does work, f times d.

38
00:01:45,763 --> 00:01:47,776
Four newtons times ten metres.

39
00:01:47,776 --> 00:01:48,778
Forty joules.

40
00:01:49,528 --> 00:01:50,428
Step two.

41
00:01:50,158 --> 00:01:51,579
That work does not vanish.

42
00:01:51,579 --> 00:01:53,915
It becomes heat, in
the block and the ramp.

43
00:01:54,665 --> 00:01:55,565
Step three.

44
00:01:55,499 --> 00:01:58,971
So U at the top equals K at
the bottom, plus the heat.

45
00:01:58,971 --> 00:02:01,527
Ninety eight equals
fifty eight plus forty.

46
00:02:02,277 --> 00:02:03,177
Step four.

47
00:02:03,024 --> 00:02:06,022
The total energy of an
isolated system stays constant.

48
00:02:06,022 --> 00:02:08,976
It changes form, but is
never created or destroyed.

49
00:02:09,726 --> 00:02:10,810
Remember this.

50
00:02:10,810 --> 00:02:12,897
Energy is never made or lost.

51
00:02:12,897 --> 00:02:14,458
It only changes form.

52
00:02:15,158 --> 00:02:16,580
Now, side by side.

53
00:02:16,930 --> 00:02:19,646
Mechanical energy is
kinetic plus potential.

54
00:02:19,646 --> 00:02:21,627
With friction, it goes down.

55
00:02:21,627 --> 00:02:24,652
Here, from ninety eight
to fifty eight joules.

56
00:02:25,052 --> 00:02:28,167
Total energy also counts
heat, sound and light.

57
00:02:28,167 --> 00:02:29,676
That never goes down.

58
00:02:29,676 --> 00:02:31,028
Still ninety eight.

59
00:02:31,628 --> 00:02:32,610
Now you try.

60
00:02:32,610 --> 00:02:35,452
A ball bounces lower
and lower, then stops.

61
00:02:35,452 --> 00:02:37,608
Is energy lost from the universe?

62
00:02:37,608 --> 00:02:38,829
Pause and decide.

63
00:02:42,029 --> 00:02:42,929
No!

64
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:46,271
It became heat and sound, in
the ball, the floor and the air.

65
00:02:46,271 --> 00:02:47,748
The total is the same.

66
00:02:48,398 --> 00:02:49,298
One more.

67
00:02:49,141 --> 00:02:51,605
A ceiling fan takes in electrical energy.

68
00:02:51,605 --> 00:02:54,322
Is all of it turned
into the motion of air?

69
00:02:54,322 --> 00:02:55,436
Pause and think.

70
00:02:58,636 --> 00:02:59,536
No!

71
00:02:58,997 --> 00:03:01,911
Some becomes heat in the
motor, and some the fan's hum.

72
00:03:01,911 --> 00:03:03,701
All of it adds up to the input.

73
00:03:04,351 --> 00:03:06,947
So where does the energy
in Diya's kitchen go?

74
00:03:06,947 --> 00:03:08,040
Follow the links.

75
00:03:08,390 --> 00:03:09,290
One.

76
00:03:08,860 --> 00:03:10,796
The gas stores chemical energy.

77
00:03:10,796 --> 00:03:13,119
Burning turns it into heat and light.

78
00:03:13,519 --> 00:03:14,419
Two.

79
00:03:13,923 --> 00:03:16,173
The heat flows into the pan and the food.

80
00:03:16,173 --> 00:03:17,638
Some escapes into the air.

81
00:03:18,038 --> 00:03:18,938
Three.

82
00:03:18,526 --> 00:03:20,584
Steam in the cooker lifts the whistle.

83
00:03:20,584 --> 00:03:22,226
Heat becomes motion and sound.

84
00:03:22,626 --> 00:03:23,526
Four.

85
00:03:23,091 --> 00:03:26,651
In the end, it all spreads out
as a little warmth in the room.

86
00:03:26,651 --> 00:03:27,880
Nothing is destroyed.

87
00:03:28,480 --> 00:03:30,170
Now, one worked example.

88
00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:33,267
A half kilogram ball
falls from ten metres.

89
00:03:33,267 --> 00:03:37,148
Air drag slows it, and it lands
at twelve metres per second.

90
00:03:37,148 --> 00:03:39,408
How much energy went into the air?

91
00:03:39,908 --> 00:03:42,107
At the top, U equals m g h.

92
00:03:42,107 --> 00:03:45,002
Half, times nine point eight, times ten.

93
00:03:45,002 --> 00:03:46,343
Forty nine joules.

94
00:03:47,093 --> 00:03:49,979
At the ground, K equals half m v squared.

95
00:03:49,979 --> 00:03:52,746
Half, times half, times one forty four.

96
00:03:52,746 --> 00:03:54,051
Thirty six joules.

97
00:03:54,801 --> 00:03:56,623
Total energy is conserved.

98
00:03:56,623 --> 00:03:59,615
So the air got forty
nine minus thirty six.

99
00:03:59,615 --> 00:04:01,487
Thirteen joules, as heat.

100
00:04:02,137 --> 00:04:03,601
Now, the exam trap.

101
00:04:03,951 --> 00:04:08,625
Many students say, energy is conserved,
so the kinetic energy at the bottom

102
00:04:08,625 --> 00:04:09,994
must equal m g h.

103
00:04:10,344 --> 00:04:12,599
But only the total is conserved.

104
00:04:12,599 --> 00:04:15,813
With friction, K plus
the heat equals m g h.

105
00:04:15,813 --> 00:04:17,129
K alone is less.

106
00:04:17,779 --> 00:04:18,964
Where is this asked?

107
00:04:18,964 --> 00:04:22,393
In board questions on energy changes,
and in numericals where friction

108
00:04:22,393 --> 00:04:23,293
makes heat.

109
00:04:23,724 --> 00:04:24,855
Exam level now.

110
00:04:24,855 --> 00:04:29,494
A one kilogram block slides from rest
down a rough curve, five metres high,

111
00:04:29,494 --> 00:04:32,445
reaching the bottom at
eight metres per second.

112
00:04:32,445 --> 00:04:35,464
It then stops in four
metres on a rough floor.

113
00:04:35,464 --> 00:04:36,364
g is ten.

114
00:04:36,308 --> 00:04:40,409
Find the heat made on the curve,
and the friction on the floor.

115
00:04:40,409 --> 00:04:41,588
Pause and solve.

116
00:04:46,988 --> 00:04:48,056
The answer is A.

117
00:04:48,056 --> 00:04:50,042
Eighteen joules, and eight newtons.

118
00:04:50,542 --> 00:04:52,745
At the top, U is m g h.

119
00:04:52,745 --> 00:04:54,748
One times ten times five.

120
00:04:54,748 --> 00:04:55,883
Fifty joules.

121
00:04:56,383 --> 00:05:00,050
At the bottom, K is half,
times one, times sixty four.

122
00:05:00,050 --> 00:05:01,297
Thirty two joules.

123
00:05:01,297 --> 00:05:03,951
So the curve made eighteen joules of heat.

124
00:05:04,451 --> 00:05:09,417
On the floor, friction turns all thirty
two joules into heat, in four metres.

125
00:05:09,417 --> 00:05:11,262
f is thirty two over four.

126
00:05:11,262 --> 00:05:12,296
Eight newtons.

127
00:05:12,896 --> 00:05:14,437
Here is your recap.

128
00:05:14,437 --> 00:05:16,021
Screenshot this card.

129
00:05:19,221 --> 00:05:21,407
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

130
00:05:21,807 --> 00:05:22,707
One.

131
00:05:22,280 --> 00:05:24,766
Name four forms of energy in a kitchen.

132
00:05:25,666 --> 00:05:26,566
Two.

133
00:05:26,112 --> 00:05:28,979
Where does mechanical energy
go when friction acts?

134
00:05:29,879 --> 00:05:30,779
Three.

135
00:05:30,434 --> 00:05:33,334
What does the law of
conservation of energy say?

136
00:05:34,434 --> 00:05:38,992
Next time: a carrom striker bounces off
a coin, but a clay ball just sticks.

137
00:05:38,992 --> 00:05:40,851
Which keeps its kinetic energy?

138
00:05:40,851 --> 00:05:42,489
We find out in collisions.
